{"hash":"51220d22c10e0a3d","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":84.918,"said":"Go down the steps into this round court, open to the sky. In the middle, on a lotus above the water, sits Adi Shankaracharya. Who was he? Tradition says he was born in Kerala in the eighth century, and left home as a boy to become a monk. He walked the length of India, teaching, debating and composing hymns; among them, tradition says, is the Stotram of the twelve Jyotirlingas that you will hear at the end of our walk. The temple committee holds that he built the temple you just walked around, beside an older shrine of the Pandavas, and revived worship in these mountains. And tradition says that here at Kedarnath, at only thirty-two, he left his body. His old samadhi shrine was destroyed in the 2013 flood; what you see is its rebuilding. The statue is twelve feet tall, about thirty-five tonnes of black chlorite schist from Karnataka. The sculptor Arun Yogiraj of Mysuru and his team carved it over nine months, and it was unveiled on 5 November 2021. Now climb back up and come round to the front of the temple. A bull keeps watch at the door, and he has a story.\n\nDid you know? The sculptor began with a block of black stone of about 120 tonnes, quarried at H. D. Kote in Karnataka.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1075},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1091,"end":1195}],"words":[[0.098,0.216,0,2],[0.326,0.3,3,7],[0.639,0.084,8,11],[0.735,0.397,12,17],[1.144,0.216,18,22],[1.372,0.216,23,27],[1.601,0.276,28,33],[1.889,0.409,34,39],[2.478,0.337,41,45],[2.826,0.096,46,48],[2.935,0.096,49,52],[3.043,0.481,53,56],[4.447,0.12,58,60],[4.579,0.072,61,64],[4.663,0.445,65,71],[5.276,0.12,73,75],[5.409,0.06,76,77],[5.481,0.457,78,83],[5.949,0.3,84,89],[6.262,0.108,90,93],[6.382,0.493,94,99],[7.091,0.312,101,105],[7.416,0.361,106,109],[7.788,0.938,110,124],[9.7,0.216,126,129],[9.928,0.24,130,133],[10.18,0.325,134,136],[11.274,0.469,138,147],[11.755,0.349,148,152],[12.115,0.108,1